  "overview": {
    "context": "Example card-game pack design",
    "summary": "This fictional card game combines multi-card packs with collecting a playable set. Each pack includes a minimum rarity, while unwanted extra copies become shards that can help craft missing cards. It is a designer's example of two reward routes working together: the surprise of opening a pack and the planned progress of crafting.",
    "distinctive": "Pack slots, duplicate limits and crafting all affect collection progress. A pack may give several cards, but its most useful contribution may be the shards needed for one specific missing card.",
    "strength": "The design interpretation is that converting duplicates into a useful resource reduces the feeling of a wasted opening. A rarity floor gives every pack a minimum promise without guaranteeing that its rare card fits the player's deck.",
    "analysis": "Explore how many packs complete the set, when duplicates stop being useful, and how much crafting shortens the chase for the last few cards.",
    "popularity": "This is not a released trading card game and has no players, reviews or published sales figures. Its mechanics represent a design pattern, not evidence that a particular real game uses these exact odds or crafting prices.",
    "business": "A commercial card game could sell packs while offering earned packs and crafting as alternative routes. The important business question is whether content and deck variety create lasting demand; this synthetic example cannot establish revenue or profit."
  }
